"executive_summary": "The direct partial oxidation of methane (POM) to methanol and acetic acid is a significant catalytic challenge due to the thermodynamic drive towards complete oxidation to CO2. This study presents a modified silica-supported bimetallic catalyst (AuFeHS) that achieves high-yield methane conversion under mild, atmospheric pressure conditions in a continuous flow reactor. The catalyst, synthesized via a modified deposition precipitation method, features atomic Fe clusters and small Au nanoparticles (1.3-3.0 nm) on a hydrophobic silica support. Characterization techniques including XPS, XANES, and HRTEM reveal that the synergy between Au and Fe is crucial, with Au stabilizing low-valent Fe species (Fe 3-δ+) that are active for methane activation. In batch experiments at 10 bar, the AuFeHS catalyst achieved a methanol yield of 1666 mmol/gFe+Au with 95% selectivity. When CO was co-fed, the selectivity switched to acetic acid, yielding 3403 µmol/gcat. In the continuous flow process at atmospheric pressure, the catalyst produced methanol with a yield of 224 mmol/gFe+Au over 4 hours, significantly outperforming previous high-pressure batch processes. The hydrophobic nature of the support was identified as critical for maintaining peroxide proximity to active sites and facilitating product desorption. Isotope labeling experiments confirmed that the methanol carbon originates from methane, not the support. This work demonstrates a viable pathway for the commercialization of methane-to-oxygenates technologies under benign conditions, highlighting the importance of bimetallic modulation and support hydrophobicity in stabilizing active catalytic species.",
